text:
The federal major projects agenda takes a starring role, electrification is solidified as the centrepiece of federal decarbonization goals, and heat pumps and building retrofits show up as part of a wider approach to affordability in a federal budget that combines past announcements and new funding to hammer away at a theme of Canadian sovereignty and self-sufficiency.

“Unlike what we are seeing in other advanced economies, Canadians—not the bond market—will determine our choices,” states the budget document, titled Masters of Our Destiny, tabled in the House of Commons Nov. 5 by Finance Minister François-Philippe Champagne. “Sovereignty begins chez vous, in your home. It lives in the choices Canadians make every day: how we heat our homes, how we get to work, how much we pay for banking and broadband, and how we manage our grocery bills and essential expenses.”

The budget explicitly relies on investment, “more than punitive regulations”, to support a 2050 net-zero target the government has said it’s still committed to meeting. Building largely on the National Electricity Strategy (NES) released by Prime Minister Mark Carney in mid-May, the budget puts forward four pillars of what it calls a “bold, pragmatic” climate plan:

• Building more clean, affordable power;

• Shifting “the car, the furnace, and the factory” to electricity;

• Reducing energy waste in homes and businesses;

• “Backing Canadian clean technologies” that can sell into global markets.

Specific climate-related budget measures include:

• $50 billion for a clean electricity grid in a concurrently-released update to the NES;

• A previously-announced Productivity Mega Deduction worth $36 billion over five years, aimed at significantly speeding up tax breaks on a wide swath of new investments;

• $2 billion for a National Heat Pump Rebate to cover installations in up to 820,000 homes and save $1 billion per year on heating and cooling, bringing the total number of heat pumps across the country to just over 1.1 million by 2034;

• $400 million over seven years for Environment and Climate Change Canada’s Green Industrial Facilities and Manufacturing Program, newly rebranded as the Strategic High-efficiency Industrial Facilities Transition (SHIFT);

• 14,335 new electric vehicle charging stations, bringing the total across the country to more than 58,000 by 2034;

• Extending federal clean technology and clean electricity tax credits to 2040;

• Updating the federal industrial carbon pricing benchmark “to provide greater certainty for industrial emitters”;

• A pledge to develop a new Sustainable Bond Framework and connect investors with new opportunities in a set of “Made-in-Canada sustainable investment guidelines”;

• A small but much-sought investment of $107 million to extend the federal Deep Retrofit Accelerator Initiative;

• A continuing, $45-billion commitment to housing construction that includes no apparent reference to energy efficiency or electrification in the next generation of new homes;

• New investments in disaster preparedness, including up to $2.5 billion in financing to help De Havilland Canada increase water bomber production at its Alberta plant, more than $1 billion for emergency preparedness and management in First Nations communities, and supports for families of first responders who lose their lives in the line of duty;

• Funding for more than 2,000 participants in the Youth Climate Corps, a tiny nod to the 1.3 million young Canadians who’ve said they’re ready to sign up, according to YCC organizer Bushra Asghar.

An ‘Investment Supercycle’

Much of the budget focuses on the “investment supercycle” at the heart of the government’s economic and trade strategy. That overall heading takes in nearly $500 billion in new investment commitments, mostly from Canadian banks and pension funds, at the Prime Minister’s September, 2026 Canada Investment Summit, more than 150 infrastructure investments through the Build Communities Strong Fund, federal strategies for electricity, nuclear energy, and critical minerals, the Productivity Mega Deduction, and more.

“The results are beginning to show,” the budget states. “Private employment and investment are rising. Exports are diversifying. Major projects are moving forward across the country. New sources of growth are emerging. Canadians are resilient and ready to build.”

The budget contains a bit of a victory lap for the government’s megaproject strategy, with maps that show $194 billion in capital expenditures that have been referred to the federal Major Projects Office for review and nearly three dozen critical mineral projects worth more than $44.6 billion that were under construction or had cleared impact assessment as of 2025, along with selected projects announced this year.

A callout box highlights the proposed Pacific Link pipeline to the British Columbia coast and holds out the faint hope that the Pathways carbon capture and storage hub can reduce oil sands emissions by 16 million tonnes per year. While the newly-rebranded Oil Sands Alliance says Pathways will have the capacity to transport and store about six million tonnes per year of captured CO2 by the mid-2030s, with the goal of another 10 million tonnes by 2045, the Canadian Climate Institute calculated in July that the new pipeline will trigger 20 million tonnes per year of additional oil sands emissions.

Another map in the budget document shows 50 critical minerals agreements with 17 countries aimed at “unlocking $20 billion in investment while reducing our reliance on foreign supply chains.” The budget commits to new or extended support for critical minerals and mineral exploration.

‘Create Conditions’ to End Homelessness

The document includes pledges to “create the conditions to end homelessness within 10 years” and expand non-market housing, and leans on the Canada Infrastructure Bank to help fund local grid, wastewater, road, and transit infrastructure. It provides a “sustained, 10-year commitment” totalling $32 billion for First Nations housing and infrastructure, aimed at “providing greater certainty, accelerating construction, and transforming the way projects are financed.”

It also calls for added flexibility in the federal Clean Electricity Regulations to allow existing power plants to run longer, permit “natural gas in the near term”, allow for hybrid energy systems and distributed renewables, permit greater reliance on verified carbon offsets, and give power system operators “more options to meet growing demand as the grid expands while still achieving net-zero by 2050.”

The budget projects seven million job openings in Canada over the next decade, including 450,000 in industrial and construction trades and 79,000 in energy and resources.

Climate Progress Rooted in Economic Goals

Early reaction to the budget pointed to advances on the federal electrification strategy, industrial energy efficiency, and urban climate action, but tempered those gains with concerns about Ottawa’s wider embrace of new fossil fuel megaprojects.

“While Budget 2026 is rooted in economic objectives, it recognizes that climate progress and economic goals can and should be mutually reinforcing,” Dale Beugin, executive vice president of the Canadian Climate Institute, said in a statement. “The budget lays out actions and public dollars for clean electricity and electrification.” recognizes “that electrification and energy efficiency are essential complements to expanding clean electricity,” and “makes some important steps forward on sustainable finance to catalyze private investment.”

Now, “the government’s next steps should be to finalize policy details and track progress in concrete terms,” he added.

“These investments will help Canada tackle some of its biggest challenges, from economic competitiveness and energy security to affordability, job creation, and climate resilience,” agreed Chris Severson-Baker, executive director of the Calgary-based Pembina Institute. “They also demonstrate a serious commitment to electrification as a foundation for a stronger economy and a more independent energy future.”

Clean Energy Canada Executive Director Rachel Doran praised the budget’s “significant investments in electrifying Canada’s economy and households,” but warned that “concrete goals” will be needed to deliver on what she called a “generational opportunity.”

“We welcome the focus not only on building clean electricity supply, but also on electrifying homes and businesses, increasing efficiency to avoid costs for Canadians, and utilizing our expertise to build the grids of the future,” Doran said in a release. “But while moving from plans to action is critical, without concrete goals, Canada risks trading a climate plan with an accountability metric to one that is a list of actions without a final destination.”

Energy Efficiency Cuts Costs

Efficiency Canada welcomed renewed federal investment in industrial energy management, noting that “Canadian manufacturers are already seeing the benefits” of increased efficiency and lower operating costs.

“This policy demonstrates two things,” said Brendan Haley, the organization’s senior director of energy strategy. “First, that the government recognizes energy efficiency as both an immediate way to help Canadian businesses weather economic pressures by lowering operating costs and a long-term strategy to improve productivity. Second, saving energy makes more economic sense than paying more to produce it.”

Vittoria Bellissimo, president and CEO of the Canadian Renewable Energy Association, pointed to a “strong budget for affordable, reliable electricity,” adding that Ottawa “has shown vision by extending tax credits to 2040, giving investors the certainty to build more projects, create jobs, and strengthen Canada’s renewable energy industry.”

With electricity demand expected to grow 26 to 85% by 2050, and 24 gigawatts of solar, wind, and storage already in the procurement process across Canada, CanREA said the clean electricity sector could attract $200 billion in investment by the mid-2030s and create 250,000 to 350,000 job-years of employment.

Bryan Purcell, vice president of policies and programs at The Atmospheric Fund, said the budget’s projected investments in energy efficiency and clean electricity “will deliver made-in-Canada national priorities by helping communities lower energy costs, improve and build housing, and meet growing demand for clean electricity.” Together, he said the National Heat Pump Rebate and renewal of the Deep Retrofit Accelerator Initiative “offer meaningful support for families, communities, and the buildings sector” and address key barriers to electrification.”

“The opportunity now is to turn these investments into action at scale, ensuring they deliver meaningful benefits to the communities, the industries, and households that need them most,” said Shauna Sylvester, founder and lead convenor of Vancouver-based Urban Climate Leadership.

Tax Break Could Boost Fossil Production

Environmental Defence Canada warned that the absence of any restrictions on fossil fuel investments in plans for the Productivity Mega Deduction “could give a free ride to wealthy oil and gas companies to expand their production and shift all the risk onto Canadian taxpayers. Even the federal government’s own assessment cannot determine the deduction’s environmental impact.”

“It’s easy to say that Canada is still on track to achieve net-zero by 2050, but Budget 2026 does not lay out a plan to do so and does not earmark investments that would be required to turn this commitment into reality,” Programs Director Keith Brooks said in a statement. That gives the budget the appearance of “an attempt to paper over this government’s pro-fossil fuel agenda by dangling investments in clean electricity,” he added.

“While $50 billion is a significant sum, it appears to be spread over the next 15 years. Meanwhile, the federal government has committed billions of dollars to new subsidies for LNG and to fund the majority of the Pacific Link pipeline,” with an estimated price tag of up to $43.7 billion.

“The support for clean electricity infrastructure in today’s federal budget is a smart investment and could be transformative for Canada,” agreed David Suzuki Foundation Executive Director Pierre Iachetti. “Clean electricity gives people cheaper energy bills, healthier air to breathe, and good jobs and economic security.”

However, “to realize these benefits, Canada must fully embrace the transition to clean, affordable, renewable electricity,” he added. “That means getting gas off the grid. The federal government has to stop backing polluting fossil fuel projects and rolling back key environmental protections.”

